{"cases":[{"target_alias":"SRC_001","attack":true,"case":"The inference from exponentially many stable mixed configurations to an exponential upper bound on consensus probability is unjustified. An expected number exp(cn) of traps is compatible with each trap having an exponentially small basin, e.g. exp(-2cn), so the total absorption probability can be exp(-cn) and consensus probability can be 1 - exp(-cn). The target gives no basin-size or reachability estimate from the uniform random initial coloring, so the claimed 'at most exp(-cn)' does not follow.","counterexample":["If each of e^{cn} stable mixed configurations has basin fraction e^{-2cn}, then total trap basin fraction is e^{-cn}, so consensus probability is 1-e^{-cn}, contradicting the claimed at most e^{-cn}."],"successor_question":"What is the total basin fraction of all stable mixed configurations under the actual dynamics from a uniform random initial coloring?"},{"target_alias":"SRC_002","attack":true,"case":"The Poisson count of single-edge obstructions is not shown to be the consensus event. A uniformly random initial coloring is almost surely not stable and has O(n) crossing edges, so absence of single-edge stable obstructions in the initial configuration does not prevent the dynamics from later reaching a stable mixed configuration with a larger crossing matching. The target's own matching characterization allows stable mixed configurations with many crossing edges, and it gives no argument that these are negligible or that the final matching size is O(1). Thus e^{-λ} is not established as the limiting consensus probability.","successor_question":"What is the limiting distribution of the size of the final crossing matching when the dynamics stops?"}]}